WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.440
Weak encryption can turn protected data into recoverable data after a breach.

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:08.980
Payment applications should avoid outdated algorithms, custom cryptography,

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.480
hard-coded secrets, and weak password hashing schemes.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:17.180
Missing or misconfigured TLS can expose cardholder data and credentials in transit.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:22.920
Secure transport requires current protocols, trusted certificates, strong configuration,

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:28.260
and careful handling across browsers, APIs, services, and third-party integrations.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.760
Poor key management weakens otherwise strong cryptography.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:37.820
Keys need controlled storage, access restrictions, rotation, lifecycle management,

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.200
and separation from the data they protect.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:45.380
Sensitive data exposure also occurs through logs, backups, exports, screenshots,

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:49.440
cached responses, and unnecessary application responses.

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:53.660
Applications should minimize what they store, transmit, and display.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:58.040
Use strong, vetted cryptography and secure password hashing.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:02.500
Configure TLS securely for all transmissions of sensitive payment data.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:06.800
Manage keys through protected, access-controlled key management processes.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:10.780
Selected PCI DSS mappings for this topic include Req.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:19.640
3.4 - Protect stored account data; Req. 3.6 - Cryptographic key management; Req.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:23.540
4.2 - Encrypt transmission of cardholder data.
